**Go down to reach leaves. Come back up to build the answer.**

One recursive pattern handles any nested object or array: walk to the leaves, then rebuild the structure on the way back — without mutating the input.

> "I need a **base case** for leaves and a **loop with a recursive call** for branches."

Write that skeleton first, then fill in the leaf and branch logic. Master it and you unlock most of the JSON / object manipulation category on LeetCode, BFE.dev, and frontend interviews.

## Leaf, branch, and three rules

Nested data is nested boxes inside boxes. Every node is either a **leaf** or a **branch**.

| Node type | What it is | What you do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Leaf** | A value you cannot navigate into — `number`, `string`, `boolean`, `null`, `undefined` | Handle directly — compare, format, filter, push, or return |
| **Branch** | A container (`Object` or `Array`) holding more nodes | Loop children, recurse, assemble from returned values |

### Three rules every solution follows

1. **Stop at primitives** — you cannot iterate on a leaf; computation must halt there.
2. **Fresh container at every level** — allocate a new `[]` or `{}` at each tier; never mutate the input tree in place.
3. **Recurse down, assemble up** — a branch has no answer until its children resolve. The parent waits, then builds the result from what the call stack returns.

## Core framework template

Same skeleton, three ways to read it — pick the lens that clicks.

Five steps. Only the leaf and branch logic change per problem.

```javascript
function universalJSONFramework(node, optionalParams) {
  // STEP 1: THE "STOP" SIGN (Base Cases)
  // At a literal leaf (primitive) or an artificial stop?
  if (node === null || typeof node !== "object" /* || artificial stop */) {
    return node; // (or return a transformed version of the leaf)
  }

  // STEP 2: THE BLANK SLATE (Container Creation)
  const isArray = Array.isArray(node);
  const container = isArray ? [] : {};

  // STEP 3: THE LOOP (Layer Traversal)
  for (const key in node) {
    const value = node[key];

    // STEP 4: ROUTING & BOTTOM-UP CONSTRUCTION
    if (value === null || typeof value !== "object") {
      // ---> [ APPLY LEAF LOGIC HERE ] <---
      if (isArray) container.push(value);
      else container[key] = value;
    } else {
      // ---> [ APPLY BRANCH LOGIC HERE ] <---
      const childResult = universalJSONFramework(value, optionalParams);
      if (isArray) container.push(childResult);
      else container[key] = childResult;
    }
  }

  // STEP 5: HAND IT UP THE CHAIN
  return container;
}
```

## Alternate reading: first principles

Same skeleton, formal lens.

```javascript
function firstPrinciplesFramework(node, constraints) {
  // 1. TERMINAL HALTING — computation must halt at an indivisible atom
  if (node === null || typeof node !== "object" /* || artificial halt */) {
    return node;
  }

  // 2. MEMORY ALLOCATION — fresh container at this topological layer
  const isArray = Array.isArray(node);
  const container = isArray ? [] : {};

  // 3. ENUMERATION — a non-terminal is strictly the sum of its children
  for (const key in node) {
    const childNode = node[key];

    // 4. TOP-DOWN PAUSE & BOTTOM-UP RESOLUTION
    if (childNode === null || typeof childNode !== "object") {
      if (isArray) container.push(childNode);
      else container[key] = childNode;
    } else {
      const resolvedChild = firstPrinciplesFramework(childNode, constraints);
      if (isArray) container.push(resolvedChild);
      else container[key] = resolvedChild;
    }
  }

  // 5. STACK FULFILLMENT — hand allocated memory up to the parent frame
  return container;
}
```

## Alternate reading: assembly desk

Picture a factory worker unpacking nested boxes — same skeleton, factory-line lens.

| Step | Factory line | Code |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Unpack** | Box or single part? | Base case |
| **New box** | Empty container of same type | `[]` or `{}` |
| **For each item** | Part → place; nested box → pass to sub-worker | Leaf vs branch in loop |
| **Seal and pass up** | Return filled container | `return container` |

```javascript
function assemblyDeskFramework(node, params) {
  // 1. UNPACK — single part? Stop and send it back.
  if (node === null || typeof node !== "object") {
    return node;
  }

  // 2. NEW BOX — empty container of the same type
  const isArray = Array.isArray(node);
  const tray = isArray ? [] : {};

  // 3. FOR EACH ITEM — part goes in; nested box goes to a sub-worker
  for (const key in node) {
    const item = node[key];

    if (item === null || typeof item !== "object") {
      if (isArray) tray.push(item);
      else tray[key] = item;
    } else {
      const finishedTray = assemblyDeskFramework(item, params);
      if (isArray) tray.push(finishedTray);
      else tray[key] = finishedTray;
    }
  }

  // 4. SEAL AND PASS UP — hand completed container upward
  return tray;
}
```

## Pattern adaptations

The skeleton stays fixed. Only leaf and branch logic in step 4 changes.

### 1. Flattening — smash it flat

Bring every leaf to the top level instead of preserving nested shape.

- **Leaf:** push the primitive into a shared array or object
- **Branch:** recurse so every child adds to the **same pile**

```javascript
// flatten: pass the SAME result array into every recursive call
```

Examples: `[1, [2, [3]]]` → `[1, 2, 3]`; `{ a: { b: 1 } }` → `{ "a.b": 1 }`

### 2. Deep comparison — two trees

Traverse two structures in parallel to verify structural and value equality.

- **Leaf:** both primitives → `val1 === val2`
- **Branch:** both composites → loop keys/indices and recurse on each pair

### 3. Transformation — translation

Keep the tree shape; change the data inside (format keys, wrap values, stringify).

- **Leaf:** format the primitive (quote strings, emit numbers, etc.)
- **Branch:** transform the key if needed, assign the recursively transformed value

Examples: `snake_case` → `camelCase` key conversion; custom JSON serializer.

### 4. Diffing / merging — conflict resolution

Given two trees, combine them or surface exactly what changed.

- **Leaf:** primitives differ → return `[old, new]` or pick the merge winner
- **Branch:** both composites → dive deeper; type mismatch (branch vs leaf) → treat as a difference

## Trace: calls down, values up

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant root as transform(root)
  participant nested as transform(nested)
  participant leaf as transform(leaf)

  root->>nested: recurse into child
  nested->>leaf: recurse into inner value
  leaf-->>nested: return processed leaf
  Note over nested: bottom-up: assemble new container
  nested-->>root: return new nested structure
  Note over root: bottom-up: assemble root container
  root-->>root: return final result
```

Walkthrough for [LeetCode 2705 — Compact Object](https://leetcode.com/problems/compact-object/) (filter falsy values):

1. **Down:** root object → nested array → leaf `1`
2. **Up:** `1` returns → `[1]` built → `{ b: [1] }` assembled; falsy `a` skipped at object level

## Pitfalls

### 1. Test the value, not the key

Property names like `"0"` or `"a"` are always truthy strings.

```javascript
// Wrong — key is always truthy
if (Boolean(key)) { ... }

// Right — test the stored value
if (Boolean(value)) { ... }
```

### 2. Don't `delete` from arrays

Use `.push()` for dense results. `delete arr[1]` leaves a hole (`[1, empty, 2]`).

```javascript
// Wrong — sparse array
if (Boolean(value)) compactedArr[i] = transform(value);

// Right — dense array
if (Boolean(value)) compactedArr.push(transform(value));
```

### 3. Guard `null` first

`typeof null === 'object'`. Without an early check, `for...in` on `null` throws.

```javascript
if (node === null) return node; // before typeof check
if (typeof node !== "object") return node;
```

### Interview angles

- **In-place vs rebuild** — immutable rebuild is clearer and avoids array holes
- **`filter` + `map` vs explicit recursion** — functional style when shape is uniform; explicit loops when base cases and container type matter
- **Recognizing tree-shaped inputs** — `JSON.parse` payloads, DOM configs, file system trees
- **`typeof null` trap** — always handle `null` before the generic object branch

